From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dennis Palmer <dodava@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 21 Oct 2026 16:50:09 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] i2c: core: Allow address 0x00 for the Surface RT covers

The Microsoft Surface RT's Touch and Type Covers are HID over I2C devices
at address 0x00, which the I2C core rejects as the general call address,
so they can't be registered. Accept it on the Surface RT only.

Based on the Open Surface RT community kernel (grate-linux,
microsoft-surface-rt branch), which makes the same exception in
i2c_check_addr_validity() and i2c_check_7bit_addr_validity_strict().
Only the first is needed to register the cover.
---
 drivers/i2c/i2c-core-base.c | 8 ++++++++
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/i2c/i2c-core-base.c b/drivers/i2c/i2c-core-base.c
index ae7e9c8b65a6..d6801bee7a20 100644
--- a/drivers/i2c/i2c-core-base.c
+++ b/drivers/i2c/i2c-core-base.c
@@ -750,6 +750,14 @@ static unsigned short i2c_encode_flags_to_addr(struct i2c_client *client)
  * are purposely not enforced, except for the general call address. */
 static int i2c_check_addr_validity(unsigned int addr, unsigned short flags)
 {
+	/*
+	 * The Microsoft Surface RT's Touch and Type Covers use the general
+	 * call address 0x00.
+	 */
+	if (addr == 0x00 && !(flags & I2C_CLIENT_TEN) &&
+	    of_machine_is_compatible("microsoft,surface-rt"))
+		return 0;
+
 	if (flags & I2C_CLIENT_TEN) {
 		/* 10-bit address, all values are valid */
 		if (addr > 0x3ff)
-- 
